================================================================================
PART A:  SINGLE-BLOCK COLORING + THREE-DISTANCE STRUCTURE
================================================================================

[A1] THREE-GAP (Steinhaus) for a block's carrier orbit.
  blocks {0..m-1} (m=2..12), rational x, 4000 samples:
     max distinct circular gap lengths = 3   (three-distance: <=3)
     violations of '<=3': 0  => A1 three-gap CONFIRMED (PROVED, Steinhaus).
  (the coloring image floor(7*pts) is a deterministic function of (frac(x), m).)

[A2] HIT-COUNT KERNEL g_r(rho) (all-singleton base case).
  g_r(rho) = inclusion-exclusion cover prob, verified r<=6: True  (PROVED kernel).
  cover6(r) [all 6 inner, r independent singletons]:
     r= 6: 0.00612  (720/117649)
     r= 7: 0.02448  (2880/117649)
     r= 8: 0.05770  (47520/823543)
     r= 9: 0.10491  (86400/823543)
     r=10: 0.16310  (6581520/40353607)
     r=11: 0.22845  (9218880/40353607)
     r=12: 0.29731  (4115105280/13841287201)

[A3] TWO full-cover objects: (a) actual p0(block); (b) carrier-averaged q.
  (a) ACTUAL p0(block) [no carrier avg] -- the genuine three-distance object:
     s=7: p0(consec)=0.14762  max bounded=0.14762 at consec  consec_max=True
     s=8: p0(consec)=0.32721  max bounded=0.32721 at consec  consec_max=True
     s=9: p0(consec)=0.41616  max bounded=0.41616 at consec  consec_max=True
  => [A3a] consec maximizes ACTUAL single-cluster p0 (PROVED-structure/VERIFIED): True
  (b) CARRIER-AVERAGED q({1..6}) [decorrelated law] -- consec does NOT win:
     s=6: q(consec)=0.03500  max=0.05258 at (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6)  consec_max=False
     s=7: q(consec)=0.20389  max=0.26244 at (0, 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9)  consec_max=False
  => [A3b] consec is NOT the carrier-averaged maximizer (HONEST CORRECTION).
     CONSEQUENCE: the multi-cluster product ceiling (C2) is taken over the per-size
     MAX carrier-averaged law -- NOT over consec.  C2 already does this, correctly.

================================================================================
PART B:  THE SMALL-|R| CONE (where consec-dominance fails) + COMPENSATION
================================================================================

[B1] CHARACTERIZE failing (shape C, residual R): q_C(R) > q_consec(R).
  count of strict failures q_C(R)>q_consec(R) by rho=|R| (sizes s=3..5, bounded):
     rho=2:   260 failures; ex C=(0, 1, 3) R=(1, 2) excess=0.00794
     rho=3:   348 failures; ex C=(0, 1, 3) R=(1, 2, 5) excess=0.03175
     rho=4:   276 failures; ex C=(0, 1, 2, 4) R=(1, 2, 3, 5) excess=0.00694
     rho=5:    78 failures; ex C=(0, 1, 3, 4, 5) R=(1, 2, 3, 4, 5) excess=0.01246
  full-cover (rho=6) failures: 0  (=> consec ALWAYS wins rho=6).
  largest rho with ANY failure: 5
  STRUCTURE (three-gap): spreading the shape widens AP gaps -> straddles more
    separated sectors (helps small spread-out R) but leaves holes -> worse full cover.

[B2] CONE COMPENSATION: context-weighted sum on GENERATED contexts.
  generated-context tests: 1500
  cone violations (focal C beats consec K in a real product context): 76
  worst excess 0.060432 at focal=(0, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8) context=[(0, 1, 3, 5, 8)]
  => [B2] consec does NOT dominate every generated product context: True.
     HONEST CORRECTION: the codex 'consecutive coherent-block quotient' does NOT hold
     for the carrier-averaged product (76/1500 fail).  The small-R cone does not
     collapse to consec.  The bound that DOES work uses the per-size MAX carrier law
     (an upper envelope, C2 below), not consec.  This REFUTES the consec-cone route
     and redirects to the max-law envelope.

